---
// One source supplies both the binary rows and their picture.
const rows = ['00011000', '00111100', '01111110', '11011011', '11111111', '00100100', '01011010', '10100101'];
---
<figure class="bit-picture">
  <div class="diagram" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="data"><span class="label">Eight rows of bits</span><div class="rows">{rows.map(row => <span>{row}</span>)}</div></div>
    <span class="arrow">→</span>
    <div class="picture"><span class="label">One small creature</span><div class="pixels">{rows.flatMap(row => [...row].map(bit => <span class:list={{ filled: bit === '1' }} />))}</div></div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Numbers become pictures. Here, each 1 fills a square and each 0 leaves it empty.
    These eight rows describe an 8 × 8 image; a machine’s display format determines how we store and draw it.</figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .bit-picture { margin: var(--space-8) 0; padding: clamp(16px, 4vw, 32px); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); }
  .diagram { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-evenly; gap: var(--space-4); color: var(--h-ink); }
  .label { display: block; font-family: var(--font-family-sans); font-size: var(--h-text-small); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .rows { display: grid; font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: clamp(14px, 3vw, 20px); line-height: 1.35; letter-spacing: .16em; }
  .arrow { font-size: 2rem; }
  .pixels { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(8, 1fr); width: clamp(96px, 24vw, 184px); aspect-ratio: 1; border: 1px solid var(--h-ink-muted); }
  .pixels span { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .pixels .filled { background: var(--h-ink); }
  figcaption { font-size: var(--h-text-small); line-height: 1.6; margin-top: var(--space-5); color: var(--h-ink-body); max-width: 64ch; }
  @media (max-width: 420px) { .diagram { flex-wrap: wrap; } .label { font-size: .7rem; } .arrow { display: none; } }
</style>
